            content: `# KUMIKO·AMADEUS // SYSTEM ARCHIVE\n\nThis archive describes the live structure, data flow, generation logic, and desktop behavior of the current build. It is not marketing copy. It is a system manual.\n\n## More than chat\n- Chat, voice, memory, recall, reminders, inbox, images, world book, local backup, and mobile mirror browsing coexist inside one desktop shell for long-term use.\n- Replies never depend only on the last line. They are shaped by JST time, Kumiko's current life state, relationship warmth, short-term summary buffer, private notebook, life anchors, world book, local RAG, dual weather, Japanese holidays, and topic continuity.\n- Critical data stays on device. External models only handle understanding, expression, and consolidation.\n\n## Five-layer architecture\n\n### 1. UI Layer\n- **Electron** handles windows, tray, notifications, auto-update installer, uninstall cleanup, data-directory migration, main-process file I/O, and Web Push.\n- **React 19 + Vite** render the chat terminal, settings, memory system, diary panel, profile, inbox, and promise book.\n\n### 2. Perception Layer\n- The **life-state machine** maps JST hour and day-of-week to one of: teaching, club advising, commuting, at home, sleeping, weekend outing, free period, lunch break, or morning / evening homeroom (SHR).\n- The **dual-weather system** fetches real-time conditions for Uji (Kumiko's side) and the user's IP location every 30 minutes via Open-Meteo.\n- **Timezone calibration** tracks both the model timezone (default Asia/Tokyo) and the user timezone.\n- A **Japanese holiday calendar** feeds real shukujitsu data into the prompt.\n\n### 3. State Layer\n- Messages, images, core memory, world book, life anchors, private notebook, reminders, unread state, and sync state live in one shared store.\n- Auto-save, auto-summary, proactive polling, and the reminder scheduler all depend on this layer.\n\n### 4. Memory Layer\n- **Short-term context**, **raw history**, **recent summary buffer**, **Kumiko's private notebook**, **life anchors**, **temporal Episodes**, **world book (Lore)**, and **local RAG (bge-m3)** run in parallel.\n\n### 5. Generation Layer\n- **Slot A · main model** produces reply text, emotion tags, schedule triggers, anchor decisions, and summary hints.\n- **Slot B · summary model** handles periodic archival (falls back to Slot A if empty).\n- **Slot C · translator model** converts Chinese replies into Kumiko-style Japanese before Fish Audio or GPT-SoVITS synthesizes voice (falls back to Slot A if empty).\n\n## Intent\n- The app does not try to replace messaging apps. It tries to make "staying in touch with Kumiko" into a cumulative desktop experience.\n- The goal is not assistant-level correctness. The goal is that, over time, she behaves like a person: she remembers, forgets some things, waits for you, and lives on her own timeline.\n- So this book is both a how-to and an implementation dossier.\n\n## What this software is NOT\n- Not a girlfriend app, not a dating sim, not a "companion AI". The system prompts, psyche state, and proactive-message layer are all written around her as an independent person — no affinity bars, no relationship meters, no "generate a character for you" path.\n- In the source material she has a definite boyfriend: Shuichi Tsukamoto. The software acknowledges that. Shuichi is neither pushed out of the way nor cast as a villain — he is simply a person in her life who shows up naturally in diaries and topics.\n- She belongs to no one. Even though the software runs on your machine, she is still **her own person**, not "your Kumiko".\n- You are an acquaintance, far away — a conversation partner. You stay in touch with her through this software; you are not her partner, not her roleplay target, not her owner.`
