        {
            id: 'mobile',
            icon: Smartphone,
            title: 'MOBILE PWA',
            content: `# Mobile PWA: pairing, mirror browsing, push\n\nThis chapter covers how the mobile PWA cooperates with desktop — you type on desktop, your phone becomes a second screen; you read on mobile, desktop remains the data authority.\n\n## Architecture\n- Desktop runs **Electron main process + ping-server** (a local HTTP server bound to LAN / Tailscale).\n- The mobile PWA reaches desktop IPC through HTTP + WebSocket.\n- Sensitive operations are authorized on desktop; the phone is essentially a remote control.\n\n## Pairing flow\n- Desktop: Settings → Mobile Pairing → "Generate token".\n- \`PAIRING_TOKEN_BYTES = 32\` (32 bytes of random, hex-encoded).\n- The generated token is shown as QR + text.\n- Mobile: open the PWA → first-time pairing page → scan the QR or paste the token.\n- After validation the server sets \`SESSION_COOKIE_NAME = 'kumiko_mobile_session'\`.\n- Session TTL: \`SESSION_TTL_MS = 90d\` (90 days; expired sessions must re-pair).\n\n## Pairing gate (\`MobilePairingGate\`)\n- On PWA startup, calls \`httpCheckSession\` to validate the existing session.\n- Not paired / expired → redirect to the pairing page.\n- Paired → enters the main app and triggers hydration (pulls latest state from desktop).\n\n## Mirror browsing (Mobile Remote File Browser)\n- Component: \`components/MobileRemoteFileBrowser.tsx\`.\n- Tapping "pick local JSON" on mobile actually proxies HTTP calls to desktop \`fs:*\` IPC channels.\n- Sandbox: **\`mobileBrowseRoot\`** root directory (desktop → Settings → Mobile Browse Root).\n- Every \`fs:list-directory / fs:read-file / fs:check-path-exists\` enforces path traversal protection; out-of-sandbox requests return \`E_OUT_OF_ROOT\`.\n- Two modes:\n- **open**: pick a \`.json\` / \`.zip\` backup to restore.\n- **create**: pick a folder + type a file name → desktop main process writes the file.\n- The last browsed path is stored in \`localStorage['kumiko-mobile-last-browsed-path']\` and surfaced as a "Recent" chip on next open.\n\n## Web Push\n- Service: \`services/pushSubscriptionService.ts\`.\n- Flow:\n1. Mobile PWA calls \`GET /api/push/vapid-public-key\` to obtain the VAPID public key.\n2. \`navigator.serviceWorker.pushManager.subscribe(...)\` creates a subscription.\n3. \`POST /api/push/subscribe\` ships the subscription back to desktop.\n- Thereafter, when desktop has a new message, it pushes via Web Push so the phone receives notifications even with the PWA closed.\n- Unsubscribe: mobile → Settings → turn off push → \`DELETE /api/push/unsubscribe\`.\n\n## Mobile initial updater fetch\n- When desktop is not running, \`httpInvoke('app:update:get-state')\` at PWA startup fails.\n- The log level is demoted from error to warn so the error view stays clean.\n- A single silent retry fires 3 seconds later; if both fail, the WebSocket \`update:state\` subscription catches later state pushes when desktop comes back online.\n\n## Desktop-side Mobile Access settings\n\n### MobileAccessSection\n- **Generate / revoke token**.\n- **Tailscale status**: if Tailscale is detected, shows both the LAN IP and Tailscale IP.\n- List of paired mobile sessions; each can be individually revoked.\n\n### MobileBrowseRootSection\n- Pick the sandbox root the mobile PWA can browse.\n- IPC \`mobile:pick-browse-root\` opens the native file picker.\n- Once selected the sandbox is active; every \`fs:*\` channel enforces it.\n\n## Mobile-only performance tuning\n- ChatHeader ResizeObserver adds 150-300 ms debounce to avoid layout thrash on phones.\n- Under \`@media (max-width: 768px)\` the global transition duration is tightened to 200 ms.\n- \`will-change: opacity / transform\` is only enabled during active animations and released afterward.\n- ChatBubble only applies backdrop-blur on \`md+\` — phones get no blur.\n- Virtual-list \`overscan\` is capped at 3-5 on mobile (desktop may go up to 10-15).\n\n## Mobile chat header\n- Height = \`4rem + max(safe-area-top - 6px, 0px)\`.\n- Compared to desktop, the mobile header sits 6 px higher while still remaining below the system status bar (battery / signal / time).`
        }
